{"id":"g.rw.boundaries.2.6f5f69ce5f","section":"rw","domain":"conventions","skill":"boundaries","difficulty":2,"group":"bnd-ne-83","passage":"<p>The poet, who rarely gave public ______ to appear at the festival.</p>","prompt":"Which choice completes the text so that it conforms to the conventions of Standard English?","choices":["readings, agreed","readings agreed","readings; agreed","readings—agreed"],"answer":"A","explanation":"The phrase “who rarely gave public readings” is extra information about “the poet.” It opens with a comma, so it must close with the same punctuation: a pair of <b>commas</b>. Mixing punctuation marks or leaving the element unclosed is incorrect."},
{"id":"g.rw.boundaries.2.9b3e7af3bc","section":"rw","domain":"conventions","skill":"boundaries","difficulty":2,"group":"bnd-ne-84","passage":"<p>The laboratory's newest microscope, capable of imaging individual ______ several million dollars.</p>","prompt":"Which choice completes the text so that it conforms to the conventions of Standard English?","choices":["molecules; cost","molecules—cost","molecules, cost","molecules cost"],"answer":"C","explanation":"The phrase “capable of imaging individual molecules” is extra information about “the laboratory's newest microscope.” It opens with a comma, so it must close with the same punctuation: a pair of <b>commas</b>. Mixing punctuation marks or leaving the element unclosed is incorrect."},
{"id":"g.rw.boundaries.2.241a9db176","section":"rw","domain":"conventions","skill":"boundaries","difficulty":2,"group":"bnd-ne-85","passage":"<p>The cave, discovered by two teenagers exploring the ______ ancient wall paintings.</p>","prompt":"Which choice completes the text so that it conforms to the conventions of Standard English?","choices":["hills, contains","hills contains","hills; contains","hills—contains"],"answer":"A","explanation":"The phrase “discovered by two teenagers exploring the hills” is extra information about “the cave.” It opens with a comma, so it must close with the same punctuation: a pair of <b>commas</b>. Mixing punctuation marks or leaving the element unclosed is incorrect."},
{"id":"g.rw.boundaries.2.71c6371877","section":"rw","domain":"conventions","skill":"boundaries","difficulty":2,"group":"bnd-colon-0","passage":"<p>The museum's new exhibit features artifacts from three ancient ______, Mesopotamia, and the Indus Valley.</p>","prompt":"Which choice completes the text so that it conforms to the conventions of Standard English?","choices":["civilizations Egypt","civilizations, Egypt","civilizations: Egypt","civilizations; Egypt"],"answer":"C","explanation":"“The museum's new exhibit features artifacts from three ancient civilizations” is a complete independent clause, and what follows is a list that specifies it. A <b>colon</b> correctly introduces such a list. A semicolon must join two independent clauses, and a comma or no punctuation fails to set off the list clearly."},
{"id":"g.rw.boundaries.2.58b1c6faed","section":"rw","domain":"conventions","skill":"boundaries","difficulty":2,"group":"bnd-colon-8","passage":"<p>The festival offers something for ______ music, local food, and craft workshops.</p>","prompt":"Which choice completes the text so that it conforms to the conventions of Standard English?","choices":["everyone; live","everyone: live","everyone live","everyone, live"],"answer":"B","explanation":"“The festival offers something for everyone” is a complete independent clause, and what follows is a list that specifies it. A <b>colon</b> correctly introduces such a list. A semicolon must join two independent clauses, and a comma or no punctuation fails to set off the list clearly."},
{"id":"g.rw.boundaries.2.5012458b73","section":"rw","domain":"conventions","skill":"boundaries","difficulty":2,"group":"bnd-colon-10","passage":"<p>The school offers three foreign ______, Mandarin, and French.</p>","prompt":"Which choice completes the text so that it conforms to the conventions of Standard English?","choices":["languages, Spanish","languages Spanish","languages; Spanish","languages: Spanish"],"answer":"D","explanation":"“The school offers three foreign languages” is a complete independent clause, and what follows is a list that specifies it. A <b>colon</b> correctly introduces such a list. A semicolon must join two independent clauses, and a comma or no punctuation fails to set off the list clearly."},
{"id":"g.rw.boundaries.2.624b7abfc2","section":"rw","domain":"conventions","skill":"boundaries","difficulty":2,"group":"bnd-colon-26","passage":"<p>The band's sound blends several musical ______, folk, and West African highlife.</p>","prompt":"Which choice completes the text so that it conforms to the conventions of Standard English?","choices":["traditions; jazz","traditions, jazz","traditions: jazz","traditions jazz"],"answer":"C","explanation":"“The band's sound blends several musical traditions” is a complete independent clause, and what follows is a list that specifies it. A <b>colon</b> correctly introduces such a list. A semicolon must join two independent clauses, and a comma or no punctuation fails to set off the list clearly."},
{"id":"g.rw.boundaries.2.bc474d00f8","section":"rw","domain":"conventions","skill":"boundaries","difficulty":2,"group":"bnd-colon-31","passage":"<p>The detective noticed one crucial ______ muddy footprint on the windowsill.</p>","prompt":"Which choice completes the text so that it conforms to the conventions of Standard English?","choices":["detail: a","detail, and a","detail; a","detail a"],"answer":"A","explanation":"“The detective noticed one crucial detail” is a complete independent clause, and what follows is a phrase that explains or identifies something in it. A <b>colon</b> correctly introduces that explanation. A semicolon needs an independent clause on both sides, and “and” wrongly joins a clause to a phrase."},
{"id":"g.rw.boundaries.2.14b7842926","section":"rw","domain":"conventions","skill":"boundaries","difficulty":2,"group":"bnd-colon-36","passage":"<p>The bakery offers three kinds of ______, rye, and whole wheat.</p>","prompt":"Which choice completes the text so that it conforms to the conventions of Standard English?","choices":["bread, sourdough","bread: sourdough","bread; sourdough","bread sourdough"],"answer":"B","explanation":"“The bakery offers three kinds of bread” is a complete independent clause, and what follows is a list that specifies it. A <b>colon</b> correctly introduces such a list. A semicolon must join two independent clauses, and a comma or no punctuation fails to set off the list clearly."},
{"id":"g.rw.boundaries.2.78aa40e16a","section":"rw","domain":"conventions","skill":"boundaries","difficulty":2,"group":"bnd-colon-47","passage":"<p>The coach gave the players one piece of ______ calm under pressure.</p>","prompt":"Which choice completes the text so that it conforms to the conventions of Standard English?","choices":["advice: staying","advice, and staying","advice staying","advice; staying"],"answer":"A","explanation":"“The coach gave the players one piece of advice” is a complete independent clause, and what follows is a phrase that explains or identifies something in it. A <b>colon</b> correctly introduces that explanation. A semicolon needs an independent clause on both sides, and “and” wrongly joins a clause to a phrase."}
